%N A399147 Number of distinct proper side-crossing points among regular polygons inscribed in a common circle and aligned by their side midpoints.
%C A399147 For each integer n >= 3, let P_n be the regular n-gon inscribed in the same circle, with the polygons rotated so that one side midpoint of each polygon lies on the same fixed radius. Let a(n) be the number of distinct proper crossing points among the boundaries of P_3, P_4, ..., P_n. A proper crossing is an intersection lying in the relative interiors of the intersecting sides. Intersections at polygon vertices are not counted.
%C A399147 Conjecture: No three or more sides belonging to distinct polygons P_3, P_4, ..., P_n meet at the same proper crossing point. Hence the number of distinct proper crossing points equals the number of pairwise proper side-crossing events. Verified numerically for n up to 1000.
%C A399147 For 3 <= m < k, P_m and P_k have common vertices if and only if val_2(m) = val_2(k), where val_2(r) is the exponent of 2 in the prime factorization of r. In that case they have exactly GCD(m,k) common vertices.
%H A399147 Eddie Lin Rui, <a href="/A399147/b399147.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 3..1000</a>
%H A399147 Eddie Lin Rui, <a href="/A399147/a399147.png">Illustration for n = 4</a>
%H A399147 Eddie Lin Rui, <a href="/A399147/a399147_1.png">Illustration for n = 5</a>
%H A399147 Eddie Lin Rui, <a href="/A399147/a399147_1.py.txt">Python program</a>
%F A399147 Conjecture: a(n) = 2*Sum_{3 <= m < k <= n} (m - [val_2(m) = val_2(k)]*GCD(m,k)).
%e A399147 For n = 3, only the triangle (P_3) is present, so a(3) = 0.
%e A399147 For n = 4, the triangle (P_3) and square (P_4) have 6 proper crossings, so a(4) = 6 (see illustration in Links).
%e A399147 For n = 5, the triangle (P_3), square (P_4), and pentagon (P_5) have 18 proper crossings, so a(5) = 18 (see illustration in Links).
%Y A399147 Cf. A399145 (vertex-aligned version), A006579 (Sum_{k=1..n-1} GCD(n,k)), A018804 (Pillai's arithmetical function).
%K A399147 easy,nonn,new
%O A399147 3,2
